WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.500
 

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.110
How accurately can we predict
the climate consequences

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:04.580
of carbon emissions?

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:07.000
How big is the uncertainty?

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.740
There is no simple
answer, but the uncertainty

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:14.210
varies greatly over the four
steps in the carbon climate chain.

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:17.640
Current emissions are known
with less than 15% error.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.670
The uncertainty in future
emissions is immense,

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:22.960
as it depends on assumptions
about what the world will

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.660
look like a century from now.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:25.700
How many people?

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:26.720
How rich?

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.130
How will they get their energy?

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:30.200
How much will they
care about environment?

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:34.180
A casual look at a 19th century
forecast of the present day

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:37.970
reveals the deep, enormous
uncertainty in any attempt

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:39.890
to predict the far future.

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:43.310
But the real question is not to
passively predict the future--

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:47.930
who's the client for that?-- but rather
to ask what kind of future we want

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:52.780
and aim there, understanding
the implications of our choices

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:56.200
for our children's future and
understanding that our children may

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:00.320
well have very different ideas about
the future they want than we do.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.730
Atmospheric concentrations are
known with very high accuracy.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.760
The concentration of
CO2 in the atmosphere

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.740
is known to much better than 1%.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:12.530
Scientists understand the
carbon budget in great detail,

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:14.420
fitting together different
pieces of science

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:18.250
to tell a coherent, well-understood
story about where carbon comes from

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:19.580
and where it goes.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:24.020
So the overall uncertainty in
predicting CO2 concentrations in, say,

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:29.700
the year 2100, is small, about 30%
if we knew the emissions perfectly.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.660
It's much harder to predict
the amount of climate change

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:36.580
for a given increase in
carbon dioxide concentration.

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:41.140
Here the overall uncertainty is
perhaps a factor of two or even three.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:44.740
That is, if CO2
concentrations are doubled,

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:48.520
temperatures might rise anywhere from
one degree, a quite small climate

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.910
change, to six degrees, a
really monstrous climate change,

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:55.130
though there is better than
2/3 chance that the temperature

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:58.940
rise would be something between one
and 1/2 and four and 1/2 degrees

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:00.750
centigrade.

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:05.310
Finally, it's harder still to predict
many of the impacts of climate change

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:08.030
on humans or the natural world.

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:11.110
Predicting the impact of climate
change on regional productivity

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:14.300
of crops like rice or
wheat is uncertain enough

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:18.940
if we had to assume that agriculture
and technology practice stays constant.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:22.890
But that's the one thing we can be sure
it will not do over the next century.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:25.440
Farmers will change crop
varieties and practices

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.540
to adapt to a changing climate.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.310
So the uncertainty is
big for all the steps.

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:34.170
And worse still, the uncertainty
cascades from step to step.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:39.870
So can we say anything useful about
the impacts of climate change in 2100?

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:41.990
The answer is yes.

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:46.140
This roulette wheel illustrates how
the future temperatures might look

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.950
if we do nothing to restrain emissions.

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:52.080
We could get lucky and have very
modest climate change in which

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:53.870
many people might even benefit.

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:59.220
Or with equally small odds, we might
get unlucky and have very rapid climate

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.960
change that was bad almost everywhere.

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:04.530
More likely the answer would
be somewhere in the middle.

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:10.160
Now this second wheel shows the
results of a specific climate policy.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.620
The climate changes are much smaller.

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:15.790
The probability has shifted
to a much better outcome.

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:19.830
There is little doubt about
which world we'd rather live in.

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:23.420
We'd like to have less chance
of damaging climate change.

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:25.820
But of course, that
leaves out the question

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:29.670
of what it costs to cut
emissions and who pays.

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:33.180
Some people argue that we should ignore
climate change because our models are

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:35.780
poor and the uncertainty is huge.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:40.770
The uncertainty is huge, but that's
not a convincing argument for inaction.

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:43.440
Uncertainty cuts both ways.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:47.140
Reality could be better or
worse than our best guess.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:51.110
It's bad policy to simply ignore
the massive changes implied

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:55.690
in the low probability, high impact
segment of the roulette wheel.

00:03:55.690 --> 00:04:00.190
The hard question is not, should we do
anything, but rather how much should

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:01.070
we do?

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:02.200
Who pays?

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:06.270
How do we coordinate our actions around
the world to achieve a shared goal?

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:09.440
This question has no
single objective answer.

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:12.760
Values and beliefs
enter in a central way.

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:16.110
In forming your own
answer to that question,

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:19.170
you have to figure out your
own view on the basic question,

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:21.269
how much does climate change matter?

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:24.550
How might it affect our lives or the
lives of our great grandchildren?

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:29.540
And how does it compare to the many
other challenges humanity faces?

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:32.870
Not all climate changes
will be bad for all people.

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:35.690
People in northern latitudes
may benefit as a growing

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:38.180
seasons lengthen for important crops.

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:40.630
Industries that want to
exploit resources in the Arctic

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:44.070
may benefit from the nearly ice-free
conditions in the summertime.

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:47.700
But there is little doubt that
most people in the natural world

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:50.350
will be worse off if
climate changes fast.

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:53.480
And it's a certainty that
if humans in this century

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:58.480
let CO2 concentrations get to, say,
three times their pre-industrial value,

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.370
they will deprive future
generations of a chance

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:05.400
to enjoy much of the natural world as
it was at the start of the Industrial

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:06.950
Revolution.

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:10.102
 

